A: I believe you may be focusing too much on the PEX API itself, or possibly some unsupported operations.
The PexContractTest in ArrayList.NUnit samples that were part of the PEx demo application demonstrates two test methods: one for the Add method, the other one for the Remove method.
But remember, the purpose of these tests is not only to prove that your methods are working, but to prove that your methods are not broken when you add or remove an item. They're designed in a way to detect "silent" failures and make sure that your tests provide confidence in your software system.
In this sense, if the behavior of your List.Remove(obj) call matches its specification (according to what the CLR specification says regarding how it should behave under these circumstances), then those tests should be all you need.
However, if your ArrayList class might behave differently, then you need to take additional steps to verify the behavior of your object. For example, instead of testing whether the count decreases by one, you could make the tests that the count does not decrease or remains the same as initially. But, of course, this comes at the cost of more complex tests, so it's a judgement call based on your specific use case and requirements.
In summary, these are the types of tests Pex might be of help:


*

*Verify that your operations do not corrupt any data.

*Verify that your operations do not overflow/underflow memory or any resources.

*Verify that your operations behave as expected under specific conditions.

*Verify that your operations have no side-effects not expected by the method's specification.


So, you don't necessarily need additional assertions or conditions to check whether an object has been removed or not from a list, you should be fine just verifying if the count has decreased or not. 
Hope this helps.
(If you're still facing issue then please provide more about the problem you are facing)
Q: Android - Set Image for a GridView Item Programmatically I'm trying to set an image for a GridView item programmatically.
In my getView() method I have this code:
convertView.setTag(new ViewHolder(imageView));

private static class ViewHolder {
     ImageView image;
     ViewHolder (ImageView image){
         this.image = image;
     }
}

and in my ImageAdapter class, I'm setting the image in this way:
ViewHolder holder = (ViewHolder) convertView.getTag();

if (imgUrl != null && !imgUrl.isEmpty()) {
     Log.i(TAG,"ImageUrl: " + imgUrl);

     Picasso.with(mContext)
     .load(imgUrl)
     .noFade()
     .into(holder.image);
}

However, I keep getting a null pointer exception when I try to set the image. This happens because the image is null.
How can I resolve this? How to properly set the image for a GridView item programmatically? Thank you.

A: If you are using ViewHolder pattern for optimization of GridView, your getView() calls can occasionally reuse the convertViews that have been scrolled off screen. 
Therefore, in your case you would get a NullPointerException, because getView() calls have received a previously used convertView with a null imageView inside it. 
You should initialize ViewHolder and imageView inside getView() method like this:
@Override
public View getView(int position, View convertView, ViewGroup parent) {
    ViewHolder holder;

    if (convertView == null) {
        LayoutInflater inflater = ((Activity) mContext).getLayoutInflater();
        convertView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.your_grid_item, parent, false); //Change this with your layout file that contain the ImageView                 

        holder = new ViewHolder();
        holder.image = (ImageView) convertView.findViewById(R.id.your_imageview); //Change this with the id of the ImageView

        convertView.setTag(holder);
    } else {
        holder = (ViewHolder) convertView.getTag();
    }

    if (imgUrl != null && !imgUrl.isEmpty()) {
         Log.i(TAG,"ImageUrl: " + imgUrl);

